    "botany": {
      "type": "editorial-story",
      "blocks": {
        "eyebrow": {
          "type": "eyebrow",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Symbolism"
          }
        },
        "heading": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Why a flower?"
          }
        },
        "lede": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Because it is how we welcome, bless, celebrate and remember — and it lasts a day."
          }
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>A flower is given at a birth, a wedding, a homecoming and a farewell. It is placed at a threshold, tucked into hair, laid at the feet of something loved. No other object carries so much feeling and asks so little.</p><p>Its only failing is that it fades. So we studied the part that matters — the turn of a petal at its edge, the way a bud holds itself before it opens — and gave it a form that stays.</p>"
          }
        },
        "button": {
          "type": "button",
          "settings": {
            "label": "The philosophy",
            "style": "outline"
          }
        }
      },
