    "craft": {
      "type": "editorial-story",
      "blocks": {
        "eyebrow": {
          "type": "eyebrow",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Inspiration"
          }
        },
        "heading": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "An Indian heart, an Italian line"
          }
        },
        "lede": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "One tradition gives us the meaning. The other teaches us what to leave out."
          }
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>From India we take the reverence — the understanding that adornment is not vanity but expression, and that a flower given by hand carries intention.</p><p>From the Italian tradition we take the discipline of form: proportion held exactly, ornament earned rather than added, the confidence to stop. Neither is decoration for the other. A piece needs both to feel finished.</p>"
          }
        },
        "details": {
